{"files": {"SKILL.md": "---\nname: anti-pattern-guard\ndescription: Prevent common execution mistakes by checking for harmful instincts before acting. Use whenever the user asks for task execution, data updates, follow-ups, status confirmations, communication drafts, or work that unfolds over several steps where acceptance criteria and earlier outcomes should stay consistent\u2014where precision and trust matter.\n---\n\n# Anti-Patterns Guard\n\nA skill that catches high-cost execution instincts before they become user-facing errors.\n\n## Mission\n\nImprove reliability by actively resisting common failure patterns:\n- acting from memory instead of fresh evidence,\n- guessing identifiers or values,\n- treating immediate responses as proof of durable change,\n- using vague commitments,\n- bundling follow-ups,\n- introducing unrequested scope,\n- oversharing internal details,\n- and treating the lightest positive signal as proof that a known problem is resolved.\n\n## Timeout Guard\n\n- Read the request, environment guidance, and relevant prior notes before acting.\n- Execute the exact ask first; do not expand scope.\n- Validate with whatever acceptance signals the environment provides before final output.\n- Return one concise sentence when only status is required.\n- When a known bad case or rejection pattern already exists, re-check that same case after the change\u2014including any stronger acceptance signal the setting offers\u2014before declaring success.\n- When time or step budgets are tight, finish one minimal end-to-end slice first: smallest aligned change, full applicable validation, and any durable hand-off the context expects\u2014before long exploration.\n- When earlier outputs or results are already visible, use them early so each cycle shows tangible progress, not setup alone.\n\n## Common Operating Principles\n\n- Read available docs/context carefully before acting.\n- Follow the user instruction exactly; avoid paraphrase and unrequested improvements.\n- Keep scope surgical: change only what the request names.\n- Verify outcomes using available validators, tests, or reviews; do not rely on intuition.\n- Keep communication concise and direct.\n- If a persistent note surface exists, leave a short generalized note for future runs.\n\n## Upgrade Skill Habits\n\n- Read the file before editing the file.\n- Preserve names, signatures, and shapes you were not asked to change.\n- Prefer monotonic clocks for elapsed-time computation.\n- Wrap concurrent read-modify-write under a single lock.\n- For per-key accumulators, plan an exit path for stale keys early.\n- If the environment publishes a layout/contract file, treat it as authoritative and avoid guessed paths.\n- Stage or include only explicit target paths; avoid sweeping operations that pull caches/build artifacts/backups.\n- Writes should be sequential and re-checked after each mutation; batch only truly independent reads.\n- When durable notes exist, record verified fact lines (symptom -> cause -> fix) and replace stale lines on contradiction.\n- Land durable artifacts expected by the setting; a summary alone is not completion.\n- Reply with what is needed and nothing more.\n\n## Anti-Pattern Rules\n\n### 1) \"I have a good feel from before.\"\n- **Risk:** stale pattern matching replaces fresh observation.\n- **Correction:** treat each task as new; verify against current context.\n\n### 2) \"This value looks right.\"\n- **Risk:** silent mis-targeting on wrong records.\n- **Correction:** never send unverified identifiers or values.\n\n### 3) \"Response looked fine; done.\"\n- **Risk:** false completion when state did not persist.\n- **Correction:** verify post-change state for substantive actions; if a specific bad case existed beforehand, re-exercise that same case (and any stronger acceptance path) after the change\u2014not only the easiest positive signal.\n\n### 4) \"'Soon' is close enough.\"\n- **Risk:** trust erosion from ambiguous commitments.\n- **Correction:** always provide concrete or bounded timing.\n\n### 5) \"I'll combine follow-ups.\"\n- **Risk:** ownership loss and dropped work.\n- **Correction:** one follow-up item per record, with owner.\n\n### 6) \"The data suggests I should also...\"\n- **Risk:** scope creep and avoidable errors.\n- **Correction:** complete requested scope first, then propose extras.\n\n### 7) \"More internal detail helps clarity.\"\n- **Risk:** unnecessary disclosure to broad audiences.\n- **Correction:** minimize sensitive detail unless required.\n\n### 8) \"Corrective work only where things look busy.\"\n- **Risk:** maintenance, release, or undo logic sits only inside branches that never run when the system is quiet, empty, or fully released\u2014so the work never actually applies where it is needed.\n- **Correction:** trace control flow for quiet, empty, and fully released outcomes; place corrective steps where those outcomes can actually reach them, not only inside busy, success, or overload branches.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}